00:10:44,980 --> 00:10:46,390
it's no longer visible in this graph

280
00:10:46,390 --> 00:10:50,750
We have completed the local and remote fit branches

281
00:10:50,750 --> 00:10:54,590
have all been grafted onto the top of the main branch

282
00:10:55,960 --> 00:10:58,480
After the previous REBASE operation when pushing

283
00:10:58,480 --> 00:11:00,000
We can see there are three options

284
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:00,520
One is push

285
00:11:00,520 --> 00:11:01,240
one is false

286
00:11:01,240 --> 00:11:03,920
the first release push is the most standard option

287
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:06,380
but since we performed the REBASE operation just now

288
00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:10,589
We need to overwrite this branch with the original one

289
00:11:10,589 --> 00:11:12,069
Their base points are completely different

290
00:11:12,069 --> 00:11:14,069
it's no longer the same branch at all

291
00:11:14,069 --> 00:11:17,480
so regular push cannot pass the validation

292
00:11:17,480 --> 00:11:19,160
It doesn't allow you to use a regular push

293
00:11:19,160 --> 00:11:21,550
so you can only use force and force release

294
00:11:21,550 --> 00:11:23,310
So what's the difference between these two

295
00:11:23,310 --> 00:11:25,210
If you are the only one

296
00:11:25,210 --> 00:11:26,610
on this branch

297
00:11:26,610 --> 00:11:29,360
others will not be involved in your branch

298
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:33,190
then using false force push is completely fine

299
00:11:33,190 --> 00:11:37,520
but using force push release is a bit safer and more secure

300
00:11:37,520 --> 00:11:41,020
if others might make modifications on your branch

301
00:11:41,020 --> 00:11:42,760
How does it provide extra safety?

302
00:11:42,760 --> 00:11:46,290
For example, let's go back to the very beginning and return to this situation

303
00:11:46,290 --> 00:11:47,890
without these four nodes

304
00:11:47,890 --> 00:11:50,570
These nodes are also not hidden.

305
00:11:50,570 --> 00:11:52,319
are displayed

306
00:11:52,319 --> 00:11:54,619
Suppose you're on B4

307
00:11:54,619 --> 00:11:58,470
someone else secretly made a change on your branch

308
00:11:58,470 --> 00:11:59,670
called B5

309
00:11:59,670 --> 00:12:01,630
This B5 was committed by someone else

310
00:12:01,630 --> 00:12:03,680
but you are still on B4

311
00:12:03,680 --> 00:12:06,000
You didn't know someone else had submitted a B5

312
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,600
then you directly rebase your feature branch

313
00:12:08,600 --> 00:12:12,630
then REBASE onto this fate branch

314
00:12:12,630 --> 00:12:14,280
it becomes like this

315
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:19,100
At this point, when you forcefully push with 'force push'

316
00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:22,449
It will first check the remote fit branch

317
00:12:22,449 --> 00:12:25,609
and whether it points to the same commit as the current fit branch

318
00:12:25,609 --> 00:12:27,409
he sees that B4 and B5 are different

319
00:12:27,409 --> 00:12:30,439
then it will prevent you from pushing to the remote

320
00:12:30,439 --> 00:12:31,639
overwrite to the remote branch

321
00:12:31,639 --> 00:12:33,619
is to prevent some smart aleck

322
00:12:33,619 --> 00:12:35,259
secretly made a commit on your branch

323
00:12:35,259 --> 00:12:38,150
thereby preventing you from overwriting its commit

324
00:12:38,150 --> 00:12:42,050
resulting in the possible overwrite and loss of his commits

325
00:12:42,050 --> 00:12:43,350
So generally speaking

326
00:12:43,350 --> 00:12:46,470
The branch for REBASE is generally your own individual

327
00:12:46,470 --> 00:12:48,350
a single-feature branch

328
00:12:48,350 --> 00:12:51,900
when multiple people are actually working on the same branch

329
00:12:51,900 --> 00:12:54,000
some people often use merge

330
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:57,270
resulting in the entire branch becoming a complex

331
00:12:57,270 --> 00:12:59,370
complex mesh graph

332
00:12:59,370 --> 00:13:02,460
rather than a perfect single-chain graph.

333
00:13:02,460 --> 00:13:05,020
At that point, REBASE is usually no longer used.

334
00:13:05,020 --> 00:13:07,720
So this situation is relatively rare.

335
00:13:07,720 --> 00:13:11,400
Of course, it's not impossible to exclude some companies that specifically adopt the REBASE workflow.

336
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:13,110
some companies

337
00:13:13,110 --> 00:13:16,990
This is the situation where REBASE is used to keep up with the main branch.

338
00:13:18,500 --> 00:13:21,750
If we want to rebase the branch

339
00:13:21,750 --> 00:13:25,010
it has already interacted with other branches

340
00:13:25,010 --> 00:13:26,730
back-and-forth merging situations

341
00:13:26,730 --> 00:13:30,900
For example, we want to rebase branch B onto branch A

342
00:13:30,900 --> 00:13:32,040
Branch A is the main branch

343
00:13:32,040 --> 00:13:36,640
then it will look for a common ancestor between branch B and branch A

344
00:13:36,640 --> 00:13:38,160
earlier ancestor

345
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:39,300
It is not A4

346
00:13:39,300 --> 00:13:40,840
but A1

347
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:47,010
then exclude all the merge nodes from these branches

348
00:13:47,010 --> 00:13:50,410
only the four branches B1, B2, C1, and C2 remain

349
00:13:50,410 --> 00:13:54,110
place these four nodes on top of this

350
00:13:54,110 --> 00:13:55,170
becomes B1 prime

351
00:13:55,170 --> 00:13:55,790
B2'

352
00:13:55,790 --> 00:13:56,330
C1 prime

353
00:13:56,330 --> 00:13:57,410
C2 prime

354
00:13:57,410 --> 00:14:01,110
Then these M1M2 are directly omitted

355
00:14:01,110 --> 00:14:04,960
During the process of replaying B1 and B2 one by one

356
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:07,280
You have to resolve the conflicts again

357
00:14:07,280 --> 00:14:09,400
It's highly likely that some conflicts

358
00:14:09,400 --> 00:14:11,400
it's not quite the same as when you resolved it

359
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:13,620
what if you have some smart aleck

360
00:14:13,620 --> 00:14:16,030
when resolving conflicts

361
00:14:16,030 --> 00:14:19,090
secretly added some new code in certain places

362
00:14:19,090 --> 00:14:22,680
then when you replay during this merge

363
00:14:22,680 --> 00:14:24,960
these things are very likely to be missed

364
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:27,400
So generally when we REBASE

365
00:14:27,400 --> 00:14:29,840
try not to discover

366
00:14:29,840 --> 00:14:33,510
This branch is no longer in a perfect linear chain shape

367
00:14:33,510 --> 00:14:34,930
then it's not a REBASE anymore

368
00:14:34,930 --> 00:14:39,640
then fall back to using merge to follow the main branch

369
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:42,280
So once you use reverse

370
00:14:42,280 --> 00:14:45,200
then everyone has to use REBASE

371
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:48,300
or everyone on your branch needs to be aware

372
00:14:48,300 --> 00:14:49,460
REBASE

373
00:14:49,460 --> 00:14:52,290
Once someone merges into your branch

374
00:14:52,290 --> 00:14:56,150
then it's likely that REBASE can no longer be used

375
00:14:56,150 --> 00:14:58,770
This branch can no longer be rebased.

376
00:14:58,770 --> 00:15:00,750
Okay, this is one point that might need attention.

377
00:15:00,750 --> 00:15:01,990
I think

378
00:15:03,940 --> 00:15:05,980
Oh, after talking so much about rebase

379
00:15:05,980 --> 00:15:08,260
Everyone must think it's very complicated

380
00:15:08,260 --> 00:15:11,820
After you graft it over, you still have to forcefully push it

381
00:15:11,820 --> 00:15:13,680
and then overwrite the remote branch

382
00:15:13,680 --> 00:15:16,730
it feels like there's a lot of communication overhead in collaboration

383
00:15:16,730 --> 00:15:18,190
From my six months of experience using it

384
00:15:18,190 --> 00:15:19,230
it feels like

385
00:15:19,230 --> 00:15:22,690
It is primarily designed for complex multi-person collaboration scenarios

386
00:15:22,690 --> 00:15:23,590
simplify the branch graph

387
00:15:23,590 --> 00:15:26,270
then it's convenient for troubleshooting nodes

388
00:15:26,270 --> 00:15:27,810
If this time

